name: dotnet-error-handling
description: Use when designing, implementing or reviewing error handling in a .NET API - Result/Error model, RFC 7807 ProblemDetails, validation errors, exception mapping, controllers and Minimal API, legacy migration, and the error contract. Works in any .NET project, with or without the dotnet-backend-architecture skill.
---

# Error handling

One internal model, one external contract. Keep it the same in every project.

## Model

`Result<T>` for expected failures; `DomainException` only for a broken invariant. There is no `NotFoundException`: "not found" is `Error.NotFound`.

```csharp
public enum ErrorType { Failure, Validation, NotFound, Conflict, Unauthorized, Forbidden, TooManyRequests }

public sealed record Error(
    string Code,
    string Description,
    ErrorType Type,
    IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string[]>? ValidationErrors = null,
    int? RetryAfterSeconds = null)
{
    public static readonly Error None = new(string.Empty, string.Empty, ErrorType.Failure);

    public static Error NotFound(string code, string description)     => new(code, description, ErrorType.NotFound);
    public static Error Conflict(string code, string description)     => new(code, description, ErrorType.Conflict);
    public static Error Validation(string code, string description, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string[]>? errors = null)
        => new(code, description, ErrorType.Validation, errors);
    public static Error Unauthorized(string code, string description) => new(code, description, ErrorType.Unauthorized);
    public static Error Forbidden(string code, string description)    => new(code, description, ErrorType.Forbidden);
    public static Error TooManyRequests(string code, string description, int? retryAfterSeconds = null)
        => new(code, description, ErrorType.TooManyRequests, null, retryAfterSeconds);
}

public class Result
{
    protected Result(bool isSuccess, Error error)
    {
        if (isSuccess == (error != Error.None))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Inconsistent Result: success and error do not match.");
        IsSuccess = isSuccess;
        Error = error;
    }

    public bool IsSuccess { get; }
    public bool IsFailure => !IsSuccess;
    public Error Error { get; }

    public static Result Success() => new(true, Error.None);
    public static Result Failure(Error error) => new(false, error);
    public static Result<T> Success<T>(T value) => new(value, true, Error.None);
    public static Result<T> Failure<T>(Error error) => new(default, false, error);
}

public sealed class Result<T> : Result
{
    private readonly T? _value;

    internal Result(T? value, bool isSuccess, Error error) : base(isSuccess, error) => _value = value;

    public T Value => IsSuccess
        ? _value!
        : throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot read Value of a failed Result.");
}
```

Rules:

- `Error.Code` is stable and namespaced: `Module.Reason` (`Order.NotFound`, `Stock.Insufficient`). Follow the repo's casing (`Order.NotFound` or `invalid_credentials`). Never use the message as the code.
- The same error always has the same code and status.
- `DomainException` is a broken invariant. Map it to 422 when it represents invalid input, or 500 when it is a bug. Pick one per project and keep it consistent.

## Contract

Every error is RFC 7807 `ProblemDetails`, content type `application/problem+json`:

| Field | Content |
|---|---|
| `type` | stable URI or error code |
| `title` | short reason |
| `status` | HTTP status |
| `detail` | human message, no internals |
| `instance` | request path |
| `traceId` | correlation id |
| `code` | `Module.Reason` |

Never a custom envelope (`{ statusCode, message, success }`). Never the status only in the body.

## Mapping

| `ErrorType` | HTTP |
|---|---|
| `Validation` | 400 |
| `NotFound` | 404 |
| `Conflict` | 409 |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 |
| `Forbidden` | 403 |
| `TooManyRequests` | 429 |
| `Failure` | 500 |

- 409 for a business conflict: duplicate, already reserved, stale state.
- 404 only when the resource does not exist; don't use it to hide a 403.
- 429 includes `Retry-After` or `retry_after_seconds` when throttling.
- 500 only for unexpected failures, never for a business rule.

Put `ToProblemDetails()` and `ToStatusCode()` in the API layer (or BuildingBlocks), never in the domain.

```csharp
public static ProblemDetails ToProblemDetails(this Error error)
{
    var problem = new ProblemDetails
    {
        Status = error.Type switch
        {
            ErrorType.Validation => 400,
            ErrorType.NotFound => 404,
            ErrorType.Conflict => 409,
            ErrorType.Unauthorized => 401,
            ErrorType.Forbidden => 403,
            ErrorType.TooManyRequests => 429,
            _ => 500,
        },
        Title = error.Type.ToString(),
        Detail = error.Description,
    };

    problem.Extensions["code"] = error.Code;
    if (error.ValidationErrors is { Count: > 0 })
        problem.Extensions["errors"] = error.ValidationErrors;
    if (error.RetryAfterSeconds is { } seconds)
        problem.Extensions["retry_after_seconds"] = seconds;

    return problem;
}
```

## Validation

- Field errors go in `ValidationProblemDetails.errors`: `field -> messages[]`.
- `Error.Validation(code, description, errors)` carries them; `ToProblemDetails()` emits `errors`.
- With controllers, `[ApiController]` already produces it; don't replace it with `string[]`.
- Business rules are `Error.Validation` or `Error.Conflict`, not field errors.

## API

Controller:

```csharp
return result.IsFailure
    ? Problem(result.Error.ToProblemDetails())
    : Ok(result.Value);
```

Minimal API:

```csharp
return result.IsFailure
    ? Results.Problem(result.Error.ToProblemDetails())
    : Results.Ok(result.Value);
```

## Unexpected exceptions

- `AddProblemDetails()`, `UseExceptionHandler()`, `IExceptionHandler`.
- Map known exceptions in one place; everything else → 500.
- No stack traces, connection strings or SQL in the response.
- Log unexpected errors with `traceId`; log expected failures at `Information`.
- Don't catch `Exception` in a controller to turn a business rule into 500.

## Legacy migration

If the project already has an error envelope or exception-based flow:

1. Keep the existing envelope until the migration is requested; don't expose two contracts.
2. Add `ProblemDetails` at the edge first; keep controllers/services.
3. Map known exceptions in one handler.
4. Convert use cases to `Result<T>` one at a time.
5. Delete the old envelope only when nothing returns it.

## Anti-patterns

- Status code in the body instead of HTTP.
- More than one error shape.
- `catch (Exception) -> 500` for a business rule.
- Flattening validation errors to `string[]`.
- Stack traces, tokens or PII in logs/responses.
- Returning 500 after the main change was committed.
- `ObjectResult` without `StatusCode` (the body says 404, HTTP says 200).

## Checklist

- [ ] One external error shape.
- [ ] Stable `Module.Reason` codes.
- [ ] Validation errors keyed by field.
- [ ] 429 includes retry metadata.
- [ ] `DomainException` mapping (422 or 500) is consistent.
- [ ] No internal details in 500.
- [ ] One place maps `ErrorType` to HTTP.
- [ ] Tests assert status, `content-type`, `code` and `traceId`.

