name: dotnet-testing
description: Use when writing, reviewing or fixing tests for a .NET backend - unit tests, integration tests with Testcontainers and WebApplicationFactory, multi-tenant isolation, transactional Outbox and idempotency, auth/session rotation, NetArchTest dependency rules, and CI. Applies to any .NET repository; architecture rules live in the dotnet-backend-architecture skill.
---

# Testing .NET backends

What to test at each level, and how to test the things that are easy to get wrong: tenant isolation, Outbox atomicity, idempotency and refresh-token rotation.

Related skills: architecture rules, tenant model and Outbox design live in `dotnet-backend-architecture`; PostgreSQL RLS setup lives in `postgres-rls`.

## Contents
- Test levels
- Testcontainers fixtures
- Integration tests per module
- API tests
- Multi-tenancy tests
- Outbox and events tests
- Auth and session tests
- Architecture tests
- CI
- Anti-patterns

## Test levels

| Level | What | Infrastructure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Domain unit | Entities, value objects, invariants, `Result` | None (pure) |
| Handler / application | One use case, real `DbContext` | PostgreSQL (Testcontainers) |
| Module integration | Migrations, query filters, interceptor, Outbox | PostgreSQL (+ Redis if used) |
| API | Endpoint contract, ProblemDetails, auth | `WebApplicationFactory` + Testcontainers |
| Architecture | Dependency rules | NetArchTest |

Rule: **do not mock the `DbContext`**. Use a real PostgreSQL container; EF Core InMemory is not representative (no relational SQL, no RLS, no concurrency tokens).

## Testcontainers fixtures

One container per test collection; share it across the whole run when possible.

```csharp
public sealed class ApiFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>, IAsyncLifetime
{
    private readonly PostgreSqlContainer _postgres = new PostgreSqlBuilder()
        .WithImage("postgres:16-alpine")
        .Build();

    public Task InitializeAsync() => _postgres.StartAsync();

    public Task DisposeAsync() => _postgres.DisposeAsync().AsTask();

    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.UseEnvironment("Testing");
        builder.UseSetting("ConnectionStrings:Default", _postgres.GetConnectionString());
        builder.UseSetting("ConnectionStrings:Redis", "localhost:6379"); // or a Redis container
    }
}

[CollectionDefinition("api")]
public sealed class ApiCollection : ICollectionFixture<ApiFactory>;
```

`Program` must be reachable from the test project:

```csharp
public partial class Program; // at the end of App.Api/Program.cs
```

Apply migrations once per container, before the tests:

```csharp
public async Task InitializeAsync()
{
    await _postgres.StartAsync();

    using var scope = Services.CreateScope();
    await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<CatalogDbContext>().Database.MigrateAsync();
    await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<SalesDbContext>().Database.MigrateAsync();
    // list every module context explicitly
}
```

## Integration tests per module

```csharp
[Collection("api")]
public sealed class CreateProductTests(ApiFactory factory)
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task creates_a_product()
    {
        var client = factory.CreateClient();

        var response = await client.PostAsJsonAsync(
            "/api/products",
            new { sku = "P-001", name = "Pork belly", price = 10m });

        response.StatusCode.Should().Be(HttpStatusCode.Created);
    }
}
```

Test the interceptor explicitly: create an entity and assert `CreatedAt`/`CreatedBy`/`TenantId` were stamped, and that changing `TenantId` on an existing entity throws.

## Multi-tenancy tests

Every `ITenantEntity` must be proven isolated. Test both the query filter and RLS (if enabled).

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task query_filter_does_not_leak_other_tenants()
{
    await SeedProductAsync(tenantA, sku: "A-001");
    await SeedProductAsync(tenantB, sku: "B-001");

    using var scopeA = ScopeFor(tenantA);
    var products = await scopeA.ServiceProvider
        .GetRequiredService<CatalogDbContext>().Products.ToListAsync();

    products.Should().ContainSingle(p => p.Sku == "A-001");
    products.Should().NotContain(p => p.Sku == "B-001");
}
```

RLS check with raw SQL using the application role (not the owner, no `BYPASSRLS`):

```csharp
var rows = await db.Database
    .SqlQueryRaw<int>("SELECT count(*) FROM catalog.products")
    .SingleAsync();

rows.Should().Be(1); // only tenant A's row, even without the EF filter
```

Also add a test that every `ITenantEntity` in the model has a query filter, so a new entity cannot be forgotten. For policy/role setup, see the `postgres-rls` skill.

## Outbox and events tests

**Atomicity** — the business row and the Outbox row commit or roll back together:

```csharp
await using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
product.Deactivate();                 // raises a domain event
await db.SaveChangesAsync();          // writes product + outbox_messages
await tx.RollbackAsync();

await using var verify = NewDbContextWithoutFilter();
(await verify.Products.AnyAsync(p => p.Id == product.Id)).Should().BeFalse();
(await verify.OutboxMessages.AnyAsync(m => m.Type == nameof(ProductDeactivated))).Should().BeFalse();
```

**Processor** — insert a pending message, run one processing cycle, assert the handler ran and `ProcessedAt` is set.

**Idempotency** — dispatch the same integration event twice and assert the effect happened once (inbox unique key or an idempotent upsert).

**Dead letters** — force the handler to fail `max` times and assert the message stops being retried and is reported.

## Auth and session tests

- **Rotation**: refresh returns a new token; the previous one is revoked and `ReplacedByTokenHash` is set.
- **Reuse**: presenting a revoked token returns `401` and revokes **all** of that user's sessions.
- **Concurrent refresh**: fire two refreshes in parallel with the same token. Assert that exactly one rotates and the other either returns the same new session or `401`, but **does not** cascade-revoke all sessions.
- **Hashing**: assert the database stores a hash, never the plain refresh token.
- **Logout**: `/logout` kills only the current session; `/logout-all` kills all.

## Architecture tests

NetArchTest must fail the build on dependency violations. Run them in a normal unit-test project with no container, so they fail fast.

```csharp
using NetArchTest.Rules;
using System.Reflection;

public class ArchitectureTests
{
    // Adjust to your modules and assembly names.
    private static readonly string[] Modules = ["Catalog", "Sales"];

    private static Assembly Load(string module, string layer) =>
        Assembly.Load($"{module}.{layer}");

    [Fact]
    public void Domain_does_not_depend_on_frameworks()
    {
        foreach (var module in Modules)
        {
            var result = Types.InAssembly(Load(module, "Domain"))
                .ShouldNot()
                .HaveDependencyOnAny(
                    "Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore",
                    "Microsoft.AspNetCore",
                    "MediatR")
                .GetResult();

            Assert.True(result.IsSuccessful, $"{module}.Domain depends on infrastructure: " +
                string.Join(", ", result.FailingTypeNames ?? []));
        }
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Modules_only_reference_other_modules_through_Contracts()
    {
        foreach (var module in Modules)
        foreach (var other in Modules.Where(m => m != module))
        foreach (var layer in new[] { "Domain", "Application", "Infrastructure", "Api" })
        {
            var result = Types.InAssembly(Load(module, layer))
                .ShouldNot()
                .HaveDependencyOnAny($"{other}.Domain", $"{other}.Application", $"{other}.Infrastructure")
                .GetResult();

            Assert.True(result.IsSuccessful,
                $"{module}.{layer} references internals of {other}: " +
                string.Join(", ", result.FailingTypeNames ?? []));
        }
    }

    [Fact]
    public void No_generic_repository_or_unit_of_work()
    {
        var assemblies = Modules
            .SelectMany(m => new[] { "Contracts", "Domain", "Application", "Infrastructure" }
                .Select(l => Load(m, l)))
            .ToArray();

        var result = Types.InAssemblies(assemblies)
            .That().AreInterfaces()
            .ShouldNot().HaveNameMatching("^I.*(Repository|UnitOfWork)$")
            .GetResult();

        Assert.True(result.IsSuccessful,
            "Generic Repository/UnitOfWork detected: " + string.Join(", ", result.FailingTypeNames ?? []));
    }
}
```

The assemblies must be loadable from the test project (reference them, or use one marker type per module with `typeof(...).Assembly`). Also add an equivalent rule so that `Contracts` does not depend on Domain.

## CI

```bash
dotnet test --configuration Release
```

- Docker must be available in CI for Testcontainers.
- Run architecture tests first (they are fast and catch structural regressions early).
- Keep integration tests in a separate project from unit tests so the fast ones can run without Docker.

## Anti-patterns

- EF Core InMemory provider as a substitute for PostgreSQL.
- Mocking `DbContext`, `DbSet<T>` or `IQueryable`.
- Testing private methods instead of behaviour.
- Sharing one database across parallel tests without isolation (use a container per collection, unique schemas, or `Respawn`).
- Only testing happy paths: the valuable tests here are the leak, rollback and reuse cases.
- Asserting on log messages instead of observable state.

# Class design (canonical shapes)

Generic shapes for the classes of **any** module. Copy the shape, not the names: `Sample` is a placeholder for your aggregate. *What* each class is comes from the module spec; *how* it is written comes from here.

`Result`, `Error` and `ProblemDetails`: see the `dotnet-error-handling` skill.

## Contents
- Rules for every class
- Aggregate root
- Entity
- Value object
- Domain event
- Command and handler
- Query and handler
- DTOs and integration events
- Query service (port)
- EF Core configuration
- Endpoint
- Domain service
- What not to create
- Checklist

## Rules for every class

- `sealed` by default. `internal` for implementation; `public` only for `Contracts` and the module's `AddXModule` / `MapXModule`.
- One type per file, file-scoped namespace.
- Domain: no EF Core, ASP.NET, MediatR, JSON or SDK types/attributes.
- No public setters. State changes go through methods that validate.
- Expected failures return `Result` / `Result<T>`; throw only for bugs or impossible states (`DomainException`).
- Async methods end in `Async` and take `CancellationToken ct` as the last parameter; forward it.
- Names: `CreateXCommand`, `CreateXHandler`, `XDto`, `IXQueries`, `XConfiguration`, `MapXEndpoints`.
- Validate in the factory or in the method that changes state — not in a setter, and not only in the endpoint.

## Aggregate root

One aggregate = one consistency boundary. It owns its children, enforces invariants and raises domain events.

```csharp
public sealed class SampleAggregate : AggregateRoot
{
    private readonly List<SampleLine> _lines = [];

    private SampleAggregate() { } // EF Core

    private SampleAggregate(Guid id, Guid tenantId, string name) : base(id)
    {
        TenantId = tenantId;
        Name = name;
    }

    public Guid TenantId { get; private set; }
    public string Name { get; private set; } = string.Empty;
    public SampleStatus Status { get; private set; }
    public IReadOnlyCollection<SampleLine> Lines => _lines;

    public static Result<SampleAggregate> Create(Guid tenantId, string name)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name))
            return Result.Failure<SampleAggregate>(
                Error.Validation("Sample.NameRequired", "Name is required."));

        var aggregate = new SampleAggregate(Guid.NewGuid(), tenantId, name.Trim());
        aggregate.Raise(new SampleCreated(aggregate.Id, tenantId));
        return aggregate;
    }

    public Result AddLine(Guid productId, int quantity)
    {
        if (Status != SampleStatus.Draft)
            return Result.Failure(Error.Conflict("Sample.NotDraft", "Only draft samples can change."));

        if (quantity <= 0)
            return Result.Failure(Error.Validation("Sample.InvalidQuantity", "Quantity must be positive."));

        _lines.Add(new SampleLine(Guid.NewGuid(), productId, quantity));
        return Result.Success();
    }
}
```

Rules:

- Private parameterless constructor for EF; private constructor with arguments for the real creation.
- `static Result<T> Create(...)` is the only entry point; it validates and raises the creation event.
- Collections are exposed as `IReadOnlyCollection<T>`, never as a mutable `List<T>`.
- No `public set`; no `Update(...)` that copies every property. Name the behaviour (`AddLine`, `Confirm`, `Cancel`).

## Entity

An entity has identity and lives inside an aggregate. It is not a mini-aggregate.

```csharp
public sealed class SampleLine : Entity
{
    private SampleLine() { } // EF Core

    internal SampleLine(Guid id, Guid productId, int quantity) : base(id)
    {
        ProductId = productId;
        Quantity = quantity;
    }

    public Guid ProductId { get; private set; }
    public int Quantity { get; private set; }
}
```

- `internal` constructor: only the aggregate creates it.
- No `DbContext`, no query, no `SaveChanges` inside an entity.
- If it needs to be loaded and changed on its own, it is probably an aggregate root.

## Value object

No identity; equality by value; immutable.

```csharp
public sealed record Money
{
    private Money(decimal amount, string currency)
    {
        Amount = amount;
        Currency = currency;
    }

    public decimal Amount { get; }
    public string Currency { get; }

    public static Result<Money> Create(decimal amount, string currency)
    {
        if (amount < 0)
            return Result.Failure<Money>(Error.Validation("Money.Negative", "Amount cannot be negative."));

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(currency) || currency.Length != 3)
            return Result.Failure<Money>(Error.Validation("Money.Currency", "Currency must be a 3-letter code."));

        return new Money(decimal.Round(amount, 2), currency.ToUpperInvariant());
    }
}
```

- A `record` gives value equality; use your `ValueObject` base if the SharedKernel provides one.
- Validate in the factory; the private constructor assumes valid input.
- No setters, no `with`-mutation in business code.

## Domain event

Something that already happened, inside the module. It does not inherit from MediatR.

```csharp
public sealed record SampleCreated(Guid SampleId, Guid TenantId) : IDomainEvent;
```

- Past tense, immutable, primitives/IDs only when it may become an integration event.
- Raised by the aggregate (`Raise(...)`), never published from a handler directly.
- If another module must react, map it to an integration event in `Contracts` (see `outbox-and-events.md`).

## Command and handler

One use case = one command + one handler. The handler orchestrates; the aggregate decides.

```csharp
public sealed record CreateSampleCommand(Guid TenantId, string Name) : IRequest<Result<Guid>>;

internal sealed class CreateSampleHandler(SampleDbContext db)
    : IRequestHandler<CreateSampleCommand, Result<Guid>>
{
    public async Task<Result<Guid>> Handle(CreateSampleCommand command, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var created = SampleAggregate.Create(command.TenantId, command.Name);
        if (created.IsFailure)
            return Result.Failure<Guid>(created.Error);

        db.Samples.Add(created.Value);
        await db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);

        return Result.Success(created.Value.Id);
    }
}
```

- Command = intent (`Create…`, `Pay…`, `Cancel…`), immutable record.
- Handler = `internal sealed`, dependencies by constructor, one `SaveChangesAsync`.
- Business rules live in the aggregate/VO, not in the handler.
- Validation of shape (required fields, lengths) can be a FluentValidation validator; validation of business state stays in the domain.
- MediatR is optional: if you replace it, keep the same command/handler shape and dispatcher contract.

## Query and handler

Queries do not load aggregates to read them. Project directly to a DTO.

```csharp
public sealed record GetSampleQuery(Guid Id) : IRequest<Result<SampleDto>>;

internal sealed class GetSampleHandler(SampleDbContext db)
    : IRequestHandler<GetSampleQuery, Result<SampleDto>>
{
    public async Task<Result<SampleDto>> Handle(GetSampleQuery query, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var dto = await db.Samples
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(x => x.Id == query.Id)
            .Select(x => new SampleDto(x.Id, x.Name, x.Status.ToString()))
            .SingleOrDefaultAsync(ct);

        return dto is null
            ? Result.Failure<SampleDto>(Error.NotFound("Sample.NotFound", "Sample not found."))
            : Result.Success(dto);
    }
}
```

- Always `AsNoTracking()` and a projection (`.Select`).
- No `Include` unless the DTO genuinely needs the graph.
- "Not found" is `Error.NotFound`, not an exception.
- Paginated lists return `PagedResult<T>`.

## DTOs and integration events

Live in `Contracts` (or in the module's `Application` if they never leave it). Primitives, IDs and enums-as-strings only.

```csharp
public sealed record SampleDto(Guid Id, string Name, string Status);

public sealed record SampleCreatedIntegrationEvent(Guid SampleId, Guid TenantId);
```

- No domain entities, no `IQueryable`, no EF types.
- Integration events are public API: version them, never break them silently.
- DTOs are read models: no behaviour.

## Query service (port)

Use it when a read model is complex, reused, or crosses modules. It is **not** a generic repository.

```csharp
public interface ISampleQueries
{
    Task<PagedResult<SampleDto>> ListAsync(int page, int size, CancellationToken ct);
}

internal sealed class SampleQueries(SampleDbContext db) : ISampleQueries
{
    public async Task<PagedResult<SampleDto>> ListAsync(int page, int size, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var total = await db.Samples.AsNoTracking().CountAsync(ct);

        var items = await db.Samples
            .AsNoTracking()
            .OrderBy(x => x.Name)
            .Skip((page - 1) * size)
            .Take(size)
            .Select(x => new SampleDto(x.Id, x.Name, x.Status.ToString()))
            .ToListAsync(ct);

        return new PagedResult<SampleDto>(items, total);
    }
}
```

- Interface in `Application` when it is a port for an external system; implementation in `Infrastructure`.
- If it only reads the module's own `DbContext`, keep interface + implementation in `Application` (`internal`).
- Name it by intent (`IStockQueries`, `IInvoiceQueries`), never `IRepository<T>`.

## EF Core configuration

One `internal sealed IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` per entity, in `Infrastructure`.

```csharp
internal sealed class SampleConfiguration : IEntityTypeConfiguration<SampleAggregate>
{
    public void Configure(EntityTypeBuilder<SampleAggregate> builder)
    {
        builder.ToTable("samples");
        builder.HasKey(x => x.Id);

        builder.Property(x => x.Id).HasColumnName("id");
        builder.Property(x => x.TenantId).HasColumnName("tenant_id").IsRequired();
        builder.Property(x => x.Name).HasColumnName("name").HasMaxLength(200).IsRequired();
        builder.Property(x => x.Status).HasColumnName("status").HasConversion<string>().HasMaxLength(32);

        builder.OwnsMany(x => x.Lines, lines =>
        {
            lines.ToTable("sample_lines");
            lines.WithOwner().HasForeignKey("sample_id");
            lines.HasKey(x => x.Id);
            lines.Property(x => x.Id).HasColumnName("id");
            lines.Property(x => x.ProductId).HasColumnName("product_id");
            lines.Property(x => x.Quantity).HasColumnName("quantity");
        });

        builder.HasIndex(x => new { x.TenantId, x.Name });
    }
}
```

- `snake_case` columns, enums as strings, `Guid` as `uuid`, `decimal` with precision.
- The tenant query filter is applied generically in the `DbContext`, not per configuration.
- No lazy loading; no `Include` by default.

## Endpoint

Minimal API, thin: bind, dispatch, map `Result` to `ProblemDetails`.

```csharp
internal static class SampleEndpoints
{
    public static IEndpointRouteBuilder MapSampleEndpoints(this IEndpointRouteBuilder app)
    {
        var group = app.MapGroup("/api/samples").RequireAuthorization();

        group.MapPost("/", async (
            CreateSampleCommand command,
            ISender sender,
            CancellationToken ct) =>
        {
            var result = await sender.Send(command, ct);
            return result.IsFailure
                ? Results.Problem(result.Error.ToProblemDetails())
                : Results.Created($"/api/samples/{result.Value}", result.Value);
        });

        group.MapGet("/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, ISender sender, CancellationToken ct) =>
        {
            var result = await sender.Send(new GetSampleQuery(id), ct);
            return result.IsFailure
                ? Results.Problem(result.Error.ToProblemDetails())
                : Results.Ok(result.Value);
        });

        return app;
    }
}
```

- No business logic, no `DbContext`, no `try/catch` for business errors.
- One `MapGroup` per module; add `.RequireAuthorization(...)` and the tenant/auth policy here.
- `ToProblemDetails()` maps `ErrorType` to the status code (see the `dotnet-error-handling` skill).

## Domain service

Only for logic that spans several aggregates and has no natural home in one of them.

```csharp
internal sealed class SamplePricingService
{
    public Result<Money> Calculate(SampleAggregate sample, Money unitPrice)
    {
        // pure domain logic: no DbContext, no I/O
        return Money.Create(unitPrice.Amount * sample.Lines.Sum(x => x.Quantity), unitPrice.Currency);
    }
}
```

- Stateless, no I/O, no `DbContext`, no `SaveChanges`.
- If it orchestrates repositories, HTTP or transactions, it is an **application handler**, not a domain service.
- Don't create one until the logic actually spans aggregates.

## What not to create

- `IRepository<T>` / `IUnitOfWork`: `DbContext` already is both.
- `SampleService` with `Create`, `Update`, `Delete`, `GetAll`: that is an anemic CRUD service; use one handler per use case.
- Entities with public setters and no behaviour.
- DTOs in `Domain`.
- `Helper` / `Manager` / `Utils` classes for business rules.
- Domain events inheriting `INotification`.
- Endpoints that query the `DbContext` directly.
- Static mutable state or singletons holding tenant data.

## Checklist

- [ ] `sealed` / `internal` per the rules above; one type per file.
- [ ] Aggregate has a private EF constructor and a `static Create` returning `Result<T>`.
- [ ] Invariants are enforced in the aggregate/VO, not in the endpoint.
- [ ] Commands/queries are immutable records; handlers are `internal sealed`.
- [ ] Reads use `AsNoTracking()` and a projection; async methods take `CancellationToken`.
- [ ] DTOs and integration events contain only primitives/IDs.
- [ ] EF configuration is `internal sealed`, `snake_case`, enums as strings.
- [ ] Endpoint only binds, dispatches and maps `Result` to `ProblemDetails`.
- [ ] No generic repository, no anemic service, no business logic in the endpoint.
- [ ] Domain has no framework dependency (enforced by the architecture tests).

